<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Required so macOS TCC permits WKWebView getUserMedia (the Panel's
         voice-input button). Without this string the OS denies microphone
         access outright, regardless of the in-webview grant wry performs. -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aleph uses the microphone to transcribe your speech into chat messages. Audio is sent only to your local Aleph daemon for transcription.</string>
    <!-- The Panel-only (lite) shell points the webview at a user-chosen remote
         aleph-server that is often a LAN address (e.g. a home server); the full
         app talks to the loopback daemon (exempt). macOS gates connections to
         local-network hosts behind this consent string — without it the OS can
         silently block the shell from reaching a LAN core. (The bundled daemon
         carries its own copy of this key; see src/bin/aleph-server/Info.plist.) -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aleph connects to an aleph-server you choose on your local network, and to search backends such as a self-hosted SearXNG or Firecrawl instance.</string>
    <!-- The Panel-only (lite) shell points WKWebView at a user-chosen remote
         aleph-server over plain HTTP (a LAN IP, or a direct host:18790). Default
         ATS blocks cleartext HTTP and does NOT exempt IP-literal hosts inside
         WKWebView, so without this the webview silently fails to load the remote
         core and the Panel stays blank. The trust boundary here is the network +
         Gateway token (see SECURITY.md), not TLS, so cleartext to the operator's
         chosen core is intentional. (The full app talks to loopback, already
         exempt — this is harmless there.) -->
    <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
    <dict>
        <key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
    <!-- The macOS install floor. It lives HERE, and NOT in tauri.conf.json's
         `bundle.macOS.minimumSystemVersion`, on purpose — that key is not just a
         plist value, it also exports MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET for the whole cargo
         build (tauri-cli's own schema says setting it to null "completely removes
         the LSMinimumSystemVersion field ... and the MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET
         environment variable"), and any deployment target >= 12.0 switches Apple's
         linker to chained fixups, which on Xcode 27 / ld-27031 lays out LINKEDIT so
         a proc-macro dylib's LC_SYMTAB.stroff lands at 4 (mod 8). macOS 26/27's
         dyld rejects that with "mis-aligned LINKEDIT string pool", rustc then can't
         dlopen the proc-macro, and the build dies as `error[E0463]: can't find crate
         for serde_derive / tauri_macros`. Measured on 2026-08-22: 10.13/11.0/11.5
         load fine (classic LC_DYLD_INFO); 12.0/13.0/13.3/13.4/14.0/15.0/26.0 all
         fail identically. rustc's own default for aarch64-apple-darwin is 11.0,
         which is why every other build in this repo is unaffected and only the
         `cargo tauri build` step broke. CI never saw it: the release matrix runs
         `macos-latest`, whose older dyld does not enforce the check.

         Trade-off, RULED ON AND ACCEPTED by the repo owner on 2026-08-22 — this is a
         decision on record, not an oversight to tidy up: the shell binary is now
         compiled against the 11.0 default rather than 13.3, so "the shell cannot call
         post-13.3 APIs" is no longer compiler-enforced; only this install gate enforces
         it. That was judged acceptable because the 13.3 floor exists to guarantee a
         WKWebView capable of oklch()/color-mix()/CSS.registerProperty (Safari 16.4) —
         a runtime WebView capability, not a symbol the shell links against. See
         interfaces/webchat/webview-baseline.json for the floor's real declaration.

         If you want BOTH the plist gate and a compiler-enforced 13.3, the direction is
         to stop proc-macros inheriting the deployment target — NOT to restore the
         config key. cargo has no clean way to express that on stable today; if that
         changes, revisit here and at edge A of scripts/check_webview_baseline.mjs.

         If you are here to "restore" minimumSystemVersion in tauri.conf.json:
         don't, unless you have verified the linker no longer mis-aligns. Check with
             otool -l target/release/deps/lib<name>-*.dylib | grep -A5 LC_SYMTAB
         and confirm stroff % 8 == 0. -->
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>13.3</string>
</dict>
</plist>
